entry moderator
term: Moderator
difficulty: intermediate
image: ../../assets/images/terms/Moderator.webp
related_terms: nuclear-reactor
related_terms: control-rods
related_terms: heavy-water
related_terms: thermal-neutron
related_terms: neutron
quick_facts: Slows neutrons from ~20,000 km/s to ~2 km/s
quick_facts: Thermal neutrons split U-235 far more efficiently
quick_facts: Light water, heavy water, and graphite are the classics
quick_facts: Lose the moderator and most reactors simply stop — a safety feature
quick_facts: Chernobyl's RBMK was the rare design that could run un-moderated

sources:
    {"organization":"World Nuclear","title":"Nuclear Power Reactors","url":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/nuclear-fuel-cycle/nuclear-power-reactors.aspx"}

definition:
    A material that slows the fast neutrons born from fission (speeds around 20,000 km/s) down to 'thermal' speeds of a couple of kilometers per second — the pace at which uranium-235 captures them billions of times more readily. Good moderators bounce neutrons without absorbing many: ordinary water (the most common), heavy water, and graphite. Water does double duty in most reactors, cooling the fuel while moderating the chain reaction.

debunking_snippet:
    “Reactors need everything to go right to stay running.” For most designs the opposite holds: lose the water moderator and the physics itself shuts the chain down — no operator required.
